-- title:
--   Local uniqueness of the Ω-coordinate of a square-zero lift
-- statement:
--   The statement is an infinitesimal uniqueness assertion for the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison map, formulated over a fixed, very large package of data; the hypotheses are grouped and named below, and the conclusion is stated in full at the end.
--
--   *Arithmetic data.* Two primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, and a non-zero level $N$ with $r \nmid N$, $\bar r \nmid N$ and $N$ squarefree (`hrr`, `hrN`, `hrbarN`, `hN`).
--
--   *The base ring.* A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring, an irreducible element $\pi$, $\pi$-adic completeness of $\mathcal O$, residue ring of cardinality $r$, and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$; together with a characteristic-zero fraction field $K_0$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   *The unramified cover.* A characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $F_r$, subject to: $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $F_r(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ (`hFr`). Further, a homomorphism $\mathrm{vdet} : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by $\mathrm{vdet}(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$ (`hvdet`).
--
--   *The indefinite quaternion algebra and its moduli.* Rationals $a, b$ such that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ satisfies $0 < a$ or $0 < b$ and is a division algebra over the completion at a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ exactly when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$; a scheme $\mathcal X$ with a structure morphism $f$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and a point assignment $\mathrm{pt}$ which make $(\mathcal X,f,\mathrm{pt})$ a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure (`h𝒳`: invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on geometrically closed points, and the universal property).
--
--   *The fine moduli problem with auxiliary level.* An integer $n \geq 3$ with $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N)=1$; a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with point assignment $\mathrm{ptF}$ on pairs (fake elliptic curve, full level-$n$ structure) which is a fine moduli space (`hM`); a group $G$ with $\rho : G \to \operatorname{Aut} M$ and labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ acting by twisting the level structure (`hρ`, the structure `IsLevelTwistAction`: the automorphisms lie over the base, twisting by $\chi(g)$ is realised by $\rho(g)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, injective and surjective modulo $n$); the forgetful morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ over the base, invariant under $\rho$, and compatible with the two point assignments (`hp`, `hρp`, `hp_pt`).
--
--   *Hecke and Atkin–Lehner structure.* For each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$ a coarse moduli scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ for fake elliptic curves with an extra $\ell$-level subgroup scheme (`h𝒴`), with the two degeneracy morphisms $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell)$ to $\mathcal X$ over the base, $d_0$ forgetting the extra level and $d_1$ computing the $\ell$-isogenous quotient (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`, `hd₀`, `hd₁`); endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base realising the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on points (`harf`, `harbarf`, `har`, `harbar`).
--
--   *The definite quaternion side.* Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$ and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ a division algebra at a height-one prime exactly when that prime lies above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ with $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1$; a finite-adelic unit $n_1$ in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ such that $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1 = R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is Eichler of level $Nr$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ into $M_2(K_0)$; a height-one prime $v$ containing $r$.
--
--   *The uniformising group.* A subgroup $\Gamma_t$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ which coincides with the group of units integral away from $v$ for $R_1$ (`hΓt`); families $s_\ell \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ and $sf_\ell$ of finite-adelic units, indexed by the primes $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$, satisfying (`hs`) that the local component of $sf_\ell$ is $s_\ell \otimes 1$ at every prime not containing $r$ and is $1$ at every prime containing $r$, that the product of the diagonal image of $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in $\mathrm{levelHeckeUSet}\,\Lambda_1\,(\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1)\,\ell$ when $\ell \mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1$ at $\ell$ otherwise, and that the reduced norm of $s_\ell$ is $\ell$; the subgroups $\Gamma_{t,\ell} = \Gamma_t \cap s_\ell \Gamma_t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`); and an element $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma_t$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   *Coordinates on the order.* The hypothesis that every integer lies in $\Lambda$ (`hΛℤ`), and a map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathbb Z_{r}^{(2)} \times \mathbb Z_{r}^{(2)}$ (Witt vectors over the field of $r^2$ elements) which is additive, sends $1$ to $(1,0)$, is multiplicative in the twisted sense recorded in `IsOrderCoord`, is injective, has dense image in each $r^k$-approximation, and computes reduced traces.
--
--   *The special fibre and its uniformisation.* A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ over the same ring (`hX₀`), two-dimensional formal coordinates $\theta_0$ for $A_0$ identifying the formal group of $A_0$ with $X_0$ through $\mathrm{coord}$ (`hA₀`), families $e, e' : \Gamma_t \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ over the base with degrees $\deg$, and the package `hE1`, `hE1mul`, `hE1sc`, `hE1'`, `hE2`, `hE3`, `hE4` (summarised here): $e(\gamma), e'(\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving level; $e$ is multiplicative and respects scalars up to powers of $r$, and only scalars of $\Gamma_t$ give multiplication maps; the formal action of $\gamma$ on $\theta_0$ is given by an element of the centraliser of the $X_0$-action whose image under any injective matrix realisation is $r^{k_\gamma}\,g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$; the action of $\Gamma_t$ on a full level-$n$ structure is labelled by elements of $\Lambda$, multiplicatively modulo $n$; and every $r$-power isogeny of a geometric fibre pulled back from $A_0$ comes from some $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ up to powers of $r$. An order $R_2 \leq R_1$ with $r$-power denominators (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`, `hR₂r`) together with $\hat e : R_2 \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ and the clauses `hE5a`–`hE5e` (summarised here) asserting that $\hat e$ is additive, $\Lambda$-equivariant and level-preserving, is multiplicative, sends $1$ to the identity and integers to the corresponding multiplications, satisfies $\hat e(\bar x)\hat e(x) =$ multiplication by the reduced norm of $x$, agrees with $e(\gamma)$ up to powers of $r$ when $x = r^{k}\gamma$, and is compatible with a single matrix realisation simultaneously for $\Gamma_t$ and for $R_2$.
--
--   *The formal moduli package.* A ring homomorphism $\iota : \mathbb Z_{r}^{(2)} \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$; a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ of height $4$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ (`hΦ`, `hΦ4`); a moduli package $MD$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`); a classifying map $\eta$ from rigidified objects to $MD$, with `hη` (three clauses): on admissible rigidified objects $\eta$ identifies values precisely with isomorphism classes, $\eta$ is natural in the base, and every section of $MD$ is locally in the image of $\eta$ on admissible objects; and an injective ring map $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-action into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is, up to a fixed power $r^m$, the set of integral matrices (`hE₀`).
--
--   *The Drinfeld identification.* A map $eD$ identifying, over each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, the points of the functor $\mathrm{ModuliPackage.G}\,\mathcal O\,MD$ with the product of the $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-points and the values of the functor $\Omega$ of Deligne data for $(K_0,\pi)$ — a Deligne datum over $B$ being a family of $B$-submodules $\mathrm{line}(M)$ of $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$, one for each full lattice $M$ in $K_0^2$, with invertible quotient, monotone in $M$, equivariant for homotheties, and non-degenerate at every prime of $B$. The identification is natural (`hnatD`), bijective (`hbijD`), compatible with the first coordinate (`hfstD`), transforms the $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-action on $MD$-points into the $\mathrm{vdet}$-twisted action on pairs (`hGLD`), sends $\pi$-translation to the Frobenius twist of the first coordinate (`hPiD`), and $\pi$-translates exist (`hPiexD`).
--
--   *Comparison of the two reductions.* A ring map $\kappa : O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), and an isogeny $\beta_0$ from $\Phi$ to the reduction of $X_0$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`). A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$.
--
--   *Atkin–Lehner data at $\bar r$ on the special fibre.* A fake elliptic curve $A_{0w}$ with morphisms $aw, aw', bw, bw'$ over the base and an integer $k_w$ such that $(aw,aw')$ realises the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ (`hALw`), $(bw,bw')$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$ preserving level (`hBSw`, `hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw` asserts that a single matrix realisation matches simultaneously the formal action of every $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ with $g\iota_0(\gamma)g^{-1}$ and the formal action of $aw$ followed by $bw$ with $g\iota_0(\bar w)g^{-1}$, both up to powers of $r$.
--
--   *The transport map $\Xi$.* A map $\Xi$ assigning to each rigidified curve over a Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebra a point of $\mathrm{GPoint}$, with: `hΞleg`, over a connected base the underlying $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-structure of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist of the given one; `hΞnat`, compatibility with base change along pullbacks of rigidified curves; `hΞiso`, invariance under isomorphisms of rigidified curves matching the rigidifications up to powers of $r$; `hΞdef`, over a connected base with $r$ nilpotent, $\Xi$ is computed by $\eta$ applied to a rigidified object $t$ obtained by transporting the formal coordinates through $\theta_0$, $\kappa$, $\beta_0$ with a twist $j \leq 1$, $t$ being admissible for the correspondingly twisted structure map.
--
--   *The normalisation $g_0$ and the comparison clauses.* An element $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ together with: `heqΓ`, translating a rigidification by $e(\gamma)$ changes $\Xi$ by the action of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\gamma)g_0^{-1}$ for some integer $c$; `heqW`, the analogous clause for the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ and the element $\bar w$, over a base in which $\bar r$ is invertible; `hK1`–`hK4`, existence, essential uniqueness, base-change and square-zero lifting of pairs (curve, rigidification) whose image under $\Xi$ and $eD$ realises a prescribed $\Omega$-coordinate twisted by $g_0^{-1}$; `hC0e` and `hC0u`, existence over connected bases and uniqueness of the height-normalised transported full level-$n$ structure determined by $P_0$.
--
--   *The comparison morphism $\Theta_f$.* A map $\Theta_f$ sending, over each Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebra, a triple (an $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-point $\psi$, a Deligne datum $P$, an element $g \in G$) to a $\operatorname{Spec}$-point of $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$; it is natural (`hΘnat`), its value is the fine-moduli point of the curve with its transported level structure composed with $\rho(g^{-1})$ (`hΘval`), and its composite with $p$ is the coarse-moduli point of the curve (`hΘvalc`).
--
--   *The situation of the statement.* Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras $B$ and $B_0$ with $\pi$ nilpotent in both, a surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map $p_B : B \to B_0$ whose kernel is square-zero in the strong form that $s t = 0$ whenever $p_B s = p_B t = 0$; an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$; two Deligne data $P, P'$ over $B$; an element $g \in G$; the hypothesis `hred` that $P$ and $P'$ have the same image in $\Omega(B_0)$ under $p_B$; the hypothesis `hval` that $\Theta_f$ takes the same value on $((\psi,P),g)$ and on $((\psi,P'),g)$; and a prime ideal $\mathfrak p$ of $B$.
--
--   *Conclusion.* There exists $c \in B$ with $c \notin \mathfrak p$ such that the images of $P$ and of $P'$ under the map of $\Omega$ induced by the localisation $B \to B[1/c]$ coincide.
--
--   This is the infinitesimal injectivity step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison between the $r$-adic formal fine moduli of fake elliptic curves with auxiliary level $n$ and the twisted quotient of Drinfeld's formal upper half plane: over a square-zero extension, two Deligne data with the same reduction and the same image under the comparison morphism $\Theta_f$ agree after localisation at any prime. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.fineFamily_lift_unique_of_value_of_squareZero_heightNormalised_conn`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.fineFamily_lift_unique_of_value_of_squareZero_heightNormalised_conn), where the local agreement is globalised to an equality of Deligne data.
